ingside and central control. If the knight moves away (e.g., to d5 or g8), we can follow up with d2-d4, gaining a big center. If Black plays d6, we can capture exd6, opening lines for our bishops and queen. This move also prevents Black from easily developing his g8 knight to f6.  \nThis disrupts Black’s development early and sets a sharp, aggressive tone, which may lead to tactical chances. In contrast, quieter moves like Nc3 or d4 are sound but less surprising. Given we already have a pawn on e4 and knight on f3, pushing e5 fits the “King’s Pawn Game” aggressive style.  \n\n**Answer:** P: e4-e5'}
